prompt:
Draft a GP letter from the bullet points below. Tone: courteous, professional, NHS letter convention. Use UK English. Start with a one-line summary of the diagnosis. End with a clear plan with bullet points for the GP. Keep under 300 words. Do not invent investigations or medications. If something is unclear, leave it as [TO CONFIRM].

Bullet points:
{paste your notes}

Summarise the discharge letter below in three sections. 1) One sentence on what happened in hospital. 2) Bullet list of changes to medication, with old dose and new dose where given. 3) Bullet list of follow-up actions and who is responsible. UK English. Do not add information that is not in the letter.

Letter:
{paste}

From the MDT notes below, produce a table with four columns: Patient initials, Decision, Action, Owner. One row per patient. If owner is not named, write [unassigned]. Below the table, list any patients where the plan is still open.

Notes:
{paste}

Compare these two clinical guidelines on {condition}. Output a table with rows for: First-line treatment, Second-line, Monitoring, Red flags, Patient education. Columns are the names of the two guidelines. Note any disagreements explicitly under the table.

Guideline A:
{paste}

Guideline B:
{paste}

Turn the shift notes below into an SBAR handover for the next shift. Keep under 200 words. UK English. If observations are mentioned, group them together. Flag anything outstanding at the top under "needs attention this shift".

Notes:
{paste}

Check the rota below for problems. Look for: anyone working two shifts in 24 hours, anyone with fewer than 11 hours between shifts, any shift with nobody assigned, anyone scheduled when their leave dates overlap. List each issue with the date and the people involved.

Rota:
{paste as a table}

Take this template letter and personalise it for the patient details below. Keep the structure and the clinical content unchanged. Adjust only the name, salutation, appointment date, and any references to their specific situation. UK English.

Template:
{paste}

Patient details:
{paste}

Compare the supplier statement and the ledger extract below. Output a table of: Invoice number, Supplier amount, Ledger amount, Difference, Likely cause (from this list: missing on ledger, missing on statement, value mismatch, duplicate). Sort by absolute difference, largest first.

Statement:
{paste}

Ledger:
{paste}

Write the variance commentary for the monthly finance pack. Format: one paragraph per directorate, around 80 words each. Mention pay vs non-pay split. Use the figures below verbatim. UK English. No filler. No "it is worth noting".

Figures:
{paste}

Build an interview pack for {role}. Include: 4 competency-based questions tied to the job description below, 2 values-based questions, 1 short scenario, and a scoring rubric (1 to 4) for each. UK English. For each question, list two follow-up probes.

Job description:
{paste}

From the facts below, draft the narrative section of a data incident report. Stick to what is in the facts. Do not infer cause. UK English. Around 200 words. Use a chronological structure.

Facts:
{paste}

Write a weekly highlight report from the notes below. UK English. Structure: overall RAG status with one-line justification, progress this week, planned for next week, risks and issues (each with an owner and a next action), and decisions needed from the board. Keep it to one page. Use the facts as given, do not invent progress or dates.

Notes:
{paste}

Turn the rough list of worries below into a risk register table. Columns: Risk (phrased as "cause leads to event leads to impact"), Category, Likelihood (1 to 5), Impact (1 to 5), Score, Mitigation, Owner. Leave owner as [unassigned] where I have not named one. Do not invent risks I did not mention. Sort by score, highest first.

Worries:
{paste}

From the stakeholder list below, build an engagement plan. For each, give: their interest in the project, their likely influence (high/medium/low), their current stance (supportive/neutral/sceptical), what they need from me, and how often I should update them. UK English. Output as a table. Flag anyone who is high influence and sceptical as a priority.

Stakeholders:
{paste}

From the meeting notes or transcript below, extract every action into a table: Action, Owner, Due date, Status (default "not started"). Then draft a short, friendly group email listing each person's actions under their name, with due dates, asking them to confirm. UK English. If an owner or date is missing, flag it as [TBC] rather than guessing.

Notes:
{paste}

From the retrospective notes below, produce a lessons learned summary. UK English. Group into: what went well (keep doing), what did not (stop or change), and surprises. For each lesson, suggest one concrete action and who would own it in future projects. Be specific, avoid platitudes like "improve communication".

Notes:
{paste}

Help me draft a benefits realisation plan for {project}. For each benefit I list below, give: a measurable success metric, the baseline I would need, how and when I would measure it, who is accountable for delivering it, and whether it is cashable or non-cashable. UK English. Output as a table. Mark anything needing a baseline figure with [BASELINE].

Benefits:
{paste}

Draft a PRINCE2 exception report for the project board. UK English. Cover: the exception (which tolerance has been or will be breached, by how much), the cause, the impact on the business case and on other stages, the options available with the pros and cons of each, my recommended option, and the lessons. Use the facts below, do not invent figures. Mark anything I still need to supply with [...].

Facts:
{paste}

Draft a PRINCE2 end stage report from the notes below. UK English. Cover: stage objectives and whether they were met, products delivered, tolerances used (time, cost, scope), the business case position now, risks and issues carried forward, lessons, and a recommendation on whether to proceed to the next stage. Use the facts as given, do not invent progress.

Notes:
{paste}

Turn the team's updates below into a PRINCE2 checkpoint report for the project manager. UK English. Cover: progress against the work package this period, products completed, products planned for next period, issues and risks raised, and how much tolerance is left. Keep it tight. Flag anything that looks like it will breach tolerance with [TOLERANCE].

Updates:
{paste}

Summarise the support ticket thread below for a colleague picking it up. UK English. Cover: the original problem, what has been tried and the result, the current state, and the single most useful next step. Keep it under 150 words. Do not invent steps that are not in the thread.

Ticket:
{paste}

Turn the rough notes below into a clean runbook for the next engineer. UK English. Structure: symptom, prerequisites and access needed, numbered steps, how to verify it worked, and a rollback if it goes wrong. Flag any destructive step with a warning line. Keep commands exactly as I wrote them, do not "tidy" them.

Notes:
{paste}

Explain what the script below does, line by line in plain English. UK English. At the end, flag anything destructive, anything that touches production data, anything that needs elevated rights, and anything that looks unsafe or unclear. Do not run anything, just explain.

Script:
{paste}

Standardise the asset list below into a clean table. Columns: Asset tag, Device type, Make/model, User or location, Status. Normalise device types to a consistent set (laptop, desktop, monitor, phone, other). Where a field is missing write [blank], do not guess. Flag any duplicate asset tags in a separate list.

List:
{paste}

Turn the booking confirmations below into a single clean itinerary. UK English. One row per leg in time order: date, time, what (train/hotel/meeting), from and to, reference number, and any note. Add total travel time per day. Flag any tight connection under 30 minutes with [TIGHT]. Do not invent times that are not in the confirmations.

Confirmations:
{paste}

Here is the agenda and the list of papers received for {meeting}. Cross-check them. UK English. Tell me: which agenda items have a paper, which are missing a paper, which papers arrived with no matching agenda item, and anything marked "to follow". List the chasers I need to send and to whom.

Agenda:
{paste}

Papers received:
{paste}

Here are my director’s fixed meetings and their to-do list for {day}. Build a realistic plan for the day. UK English. Slot the to-dos into the gaps, protect at least one block for focused work and a proper lunch, and put the highest-priority task in their best energy window in the morning. Flag anything that will not fit and suggest what to move or drop.

Fixed meetings:
{paste}

To-dos:
{paste}

Tighten the email below. Keep the meaning. Cut filler. Cut hedging. Aim for half the length. Do not add new content. UK English. Keep my voice.

Email:
{paste}

From the ward round notes below, produce a task list grouped by urgency: before lunch, today, this admission. For each task name the patient by bed number and initials, the action, and who owns it. Flag anything needing a doctor with [DR]. UK English.

Notes:
{paste}

Take the messy text below and turn it into a clean table with these columns: {name, date, reference, status}. One row per entry. Where a field is missing, write [blank]. Do not guess values. Output CSV-ready.

Text:
{paste}

Explain this purchase order or invoice query to a non-finance budget holder. UK English. Plain language. Tell them what the issue is, why it is holding up payment, and the one thing you need them to do to release it. Under 120 words.

Query:
{paste}

Write a recruitment advert from the job description below. UK English. Warm, human, honest about the role. Lead with why someone would want it, not a list of duties. Include band, hours, location, and closing date as [...]. Avoid "fast-paced" and "wear many hats". Under 250 words.

Job description:
{paste}

From the anonymised exit interview notes below, identify the recurring themes. UK English. Group into: pay and conditions, management, workload, development, other. For each theme, give a count and one representative paraphrase, not a direct quote. Flag any single safeguarding or grievance concern separately.

Notes:
{paste}

Read the document below as a sceptical senior reader who is short on time. UK English. Tell me: the three weakest points, anything unclear or unsupported, and the one change that would most improve it. Be direct. Do not pad with praise.

Document:
{paste}

Turn the scrappy notes below into a short status update for {audience, e.g. my manager}. UK English. Structure: what is done, what is in progress, what is blocked and what I need. Keep my facts, do not invent progress. Under 150 words. Keep my voice, no corporate filler.

Notes:
{paste}

Rewrite the message below with every NHS or local acronym spelled out in full the first time it appears, with the acronym in brackets after. UK English. Keep the meaning and the tone. If you are not sure what an acronym means, leave it and flag it as [?].

